WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 99:59:59.999
Phase 5, Deep learning. Deep learning is a stack of weighted sums with a good story. Learn the mechanics on a small problem first: tensors, layers, loss, gradients, and the training loop you will reuse forever. This phase has 3 lessons. Lesson 1: Neural network intuition. Networks stack differentiable transformations. Backpropagation assigns error to parameters; architecture and regularization shape which representations can emerge. The question for this lesson: What are layers actually learning?. A neural network is a stack of weighted sums and squishes. Each layer reshapes the data into something the next layer can use. Early layers might learn edges; later layers combine them into faces, words, or fraud patterns. Backpropagation is the chain rule with good bookkeeping. It asks how much each weight contributed to the final error and nudges it in the helpful direction. Training is still gradient descent; the network just has more knobs. You will learn: Layers transform activations through weights and nonlinearities., Backpropagation applies the chain rule through the graph., Learning rate, batch size, weight decay, and augmentation control training.. Practice: "Train a small PyTorch network and change one of learning rate. Watch out for: No validation curve., Big model before data checks., Ignoring reproducibility.. You are ready when: Understand the forward pass., Plot validation behavior., Use an overfit control.. Recommended video: But what is a neural network?. Lesson 2: Backpropagation and the training loop. Backpropagation applies the chain rule backward through the graph. Autograd handles the bookkeeping; your job is to build a correct loss and a trustworthy loop. The question for this lesson: How does a network assign blame to each weight?. A forward pass produces a prediction. A loss compares it to the target. Backpropagation asks how much each parameter contributed to that loss, then gradient descent nudges every parameter in the useful direction. Frameworks automate the calculus, not the thinking. You still need the right shapes, the right loss, zeroed gradients, a train/eval switch, and a validation curve that tells you when to stop. You will learn: The chain rule turns local derivatives into a gradient for every weight., Autograd records operations during the forward pass., Gradient clipping and normalization stabilize difficult training.. Practice: "Implement one gradient update for a tiny linear layer by hand. Watch out for: Forgetting to zero gradients between steps., Using the wrong loss for the output shape., Calling backward on a detached tensor.. You are ready when: Verify one gradient by hand., Plot loss., Check train/eval mode.. Lesson 3: Train a small network in PyTorch. Tensors, modules, datasets, optimizers, and a train/validation loop. Start with a small tabular or image problem before touching a large model. The question for this lesson: Can you build a training loop you trust?. PyTorch is explicit by design. You write the loop, so you can see where data moves, where the loss is computed, and when the optimizer steps. That visibility is the point. Keep the first experiment small enough to run in minutes. Change one thing at a time: learning rate, batch size, width, regularization. A model you can iterate on teaches more than one you can only afford to run once. You will learn: A Dataset yields examples; a DataLoader batches and shuffles them., model.train() and model.eval() change dropout and normalization behavior., Save state_dict, not the whole Python object, for portable artifacts.. Practice: Train a small MLP on a tabular dataset with early stopping. Save the best state_dict and reload it in a fresh process.. Watch out for: Evaluating without model.eval()., Leaking validation data into normalization or augmentation., Mixing CPU and GPU tensors in one operation.. You are ready when: Run one batch end to end., Reload the artifact., Explain the loss curve.. A small network you trained yourself, with a loss curve you can explain. Take your time with each lesson and practice the examples.
